the breast cancer deaths have declined  however it is still the second most  common cancer among women in the U.S but  it is the leading cause of cancer deaths  among Hispanic women Denise Valdez tells  us more about the increased cases in our  buddy check reports  Dr R Salon Solomon is with Optum care  Breast Care the latest CDC numbers show  Hispanic women are diagnosed in slightly  higher numbers The Five-Year breast  cancer survival rate for Latino women  tends to be lowered and Caucasian women  by about four percent so we're looking  at 92 percent versus 88 percent plus  it's more likely to be discovered at a  later stage Hispanic women have higher  odds than non-hispanic white women to be  diagnosed with tumors that are larger  and hormone receptor negative making it  tougher to treat once your tumor becomes  larger or your lymph node becomes  positive then you're hot you're more  likely to have a later stage cancer  invasive breast cancer is the most  commonly diagnosed cancer and the  leading cause of cancer death among  Hispanic women so awareness is key Dr  Solomon says one way to overcome it is  to have conversations at home so so I  think that's the first place that it can  start where we raise awareness within  our own families you know to get your  mom or your sister or your grandma to go  get their mammogram that's what we try  to do here every month we encourage you  to do your breast self exam either in  the shower or laying down because nobody  knows their body better than you and  don't put off that annual screening  either like all cancers it's best to  catch it early for Buddy check Denise  Valdez
 

